 17592186045417] [17592186110018 #edb/ref 1080 "x = \\frac{1}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110018 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110018 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d162fd51-dc22-55af-af67-6651e200040f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110019 #edb/ref 1080 "x = 5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b1d0a63-831c-5a81-82ba-af5812a26e70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110020 #edb/ref 1080 "x = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110020 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110020 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b2ca5af-5112-5fc5-a29c-bf8dcbd0c25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110021 #edb/ref 1080 "x = 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110021 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110021 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4cb287a2-00eb-5f92-afc1-1919b8f8b5fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110022 #edb/ref 1080 "x =-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110022 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110022 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6fcf43d0-22c7-50ca-a979-2cfa00df8fca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110302 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110302 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y = \\log (x)$?\n\n![](images/5f/5f38ded6da0c01657574cf0a40d86d2c5d2ce6b9ff1c29ceaaefbbe82103a277.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb79d4e2-53ee-5a09-93ad-0b2e1bdc645e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110302 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\log (x)$:\n\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n- For $x>1$, the function is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x$. We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty < y < \\infty$. The function is not defined at $x=0$.\n- The function has the vertical asymptote $x=0$.\n- The function is an increasing function.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that displays all of these properties is II. (For example, plots I, III, and IV all do not have a vertical asymptote $x=0$.)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110303 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110303 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the domain and range of the function $y=\\ln x$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110303 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "428c177d-8b83-50ad-9840-0dd3ffdf14ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110303 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y = \\ln x$.\n\n![](images/00/004c1050017c1a0631d0dda46c8335248b4f05dad15645513b71e88588d4ed0c.png)\n\nThe domain of $y=\\ln x$ is $x \\in (0,\\infty)$ and the range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110304 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110304 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\ln(x)$?\n\n1. $y\\rightarrow-\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow 0^+$\n2. The curve passes through the point $(1,0)$\n3. $y\\rightarrow\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b162bb35-509f-5ed3-896c-19eb9c52deb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110304 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\ln (x)$.\n\n![](images/d9/d9ca282050c64ffc1ab1950dd6d89288e6d65d680e45bbc9ef29c1a4ae2c3a67.png)\n\nNow, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $0^+$, $y$ decreases without bound.\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve gives a result of $0$:\n$y|= \\ln (1) = 0✓$\n- Statement III is false. The function is not defined for $x<0$, so the function is not defined as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110305 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110305 #edb/ref 1071 